[0.0, "o", "38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m \u001b(B\u001b[0;1m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m**The motive is largely American.**\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m As I said, the best argument is getting around grid   \r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:126:129:136m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m   \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:90:101:125m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m↪\u001b[9;5H \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64mconnection queues and permitting, not cheaper power. That bottleneck is mainly a US       \r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:126:129:136m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m   \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:90:101:125m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m↪\u001b[10;5H \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64mproblem. China doesn't have it to the same degree: it has 339 gigawatts of wind and solar \r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:126:129:136m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m   \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:90:101:125m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m↪\u001b[11;5H \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64mcapacity under construction —\u001b[11;35H roughly two-thirds of the world's total. So a country with  \r\n\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:126:129:136m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m   \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:90:101:125m\u001b[48:2:46:52:64m↪\u001b[12;5H \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38:2:205:206:207m"]
